WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
Submilli maintains public curated packages
for common services.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.340
You can also write custom packages for
your own systems.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.660
Both are Submilli code. A package gives
the agent’s program

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.900
a typed business operation. Here,
applyCredit takes a

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.420
customer ID and an amount, and returns a
Credit.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.940
The capability annotation names the
operation and declares the fields

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.060
available to a blueprint rule: customer,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.300
account class, and amount. Those fields
describe what this call

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.500
means. The annotation is a contract,

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.900
not enforcement. The explicit check is the
part that asks the runtime

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:41.940
for permission.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.440
The package validates the amount,
normalizes the customer ID,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.640
and looks up the account class. It passes
those facts to check.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.960
The same normalized ID is used for the
credit.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.200
The metadata lookup happens first; the
permission check must come

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.440
before the protected credit action.

00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:07.533
The application binds Customer A's ID as
trusted context.

00:01:07.613 --> 00:01:11.453
This rule allows a premium credit only
when the requested customer

00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:15.533
matches that ID. Premium Customer A
matches.

00:01:15.773 --> 00:01:20.333
Premium Customer B does not. The package
supplies the facts;

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.013
the runtime decides.

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:26.747
Packages use the same Submilli TypeScript
subset.

00:01:27.027 --> 00:01:31.267
They build on other packages and built-in
modules such as HTTP.

00:01:31.907 --> 00:01:35.267
Dependencies must be available when the
package is built,

00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:38.987
and the blueprint must grant the access
each caller needs.

00:01:39.347 --> 00:01:43.427
After the credit check succeeds, the
billing package uses its permitted

00:01:43.587 --> 00:01:47.507
HTTP and secret access to contact the
billing service.

00:01:47.987 --> 00:01:50.067
It keeps the credential internal.

00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:54.893
Build checks compare the declaration with
the check and derive the

00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:58.293
package’s provided capabilities and
required access.

00:01:58.773 --> 00:02:02.853
Review those together. Test allowed and
denied inputs,

00:02:03.093 --> 00:02:07.453
including another customer, and verify
that denial prevents the credit.

00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:11.733
The package exposes a useful operation and
keeps the credit action

00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:14.053
behind an explicit permission check.
